# `MEMBERSHIP-KEY-ACCEPTANCE`: `in=` is WITHDRAWN from `statechart`, and the argument
# here is NOT the flowchart one. It is a RESERVATION.
#
# `statechart` declares no subject vocabulary at all (`SUBJECT-VOCABULARY-SCOPE`), so the `group` id
# that was `in=`'s only value domain cannot exist here and every value was a
# dead end — that much is the same. What decided it is the future: `in=`'s
# value domain in THIS genre is a `state` id, not a group id. UML 2.5.1
# §14.2.3.4 composite states are states nested inside a state, and they would
# arrive as nesting on `state`, through this exact key. A key left live with a
# group-id domain therefore taught the WRONG model using the very spelling
# reserved for the right one — worse than an error and worse than silence.
#
# The fixture writes `in=idle`, whose value IS a declared state id, because
# that is precisely the form the reservation is about: it is refused today,
# and the diagnostic says what would bring it back.
#
# `open` is declared and connected on its own lines, and the refused `in=`
# sits on a THIRD state nothing references — deliberately, so the golden holds
# ONE error. A line that errors abandons its declaration, so writing `in=` on a
# state a transition names would add an `unknown endpoint` cascade whose
# subject is error RECOVERY (conformance/README.md, exception 2), not this
# withdrawal.
figdown 0.2 statechart
state idle "IDLE"
state open "OPEN"
transition idle -> open
state up "UP" in=open
